## Document Boundaries

| File | Focus | Do not put here |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/00-intake/README.md` | background, users, goals, constraints | concrete technical design |
| `docs/01-requirements/README.md` | what / why / success | framework choices, tables, endpoint implementation |
| `docs/02-design/README.md` | how / architecture / data / interface | task execution order |
| `docs/03-implementation/README.md` | milestones, dependencies, delivery order | low-level implementation detail |
| `docs/04-tdd/README.md` | how requirements are verified | vague “write tests later” statements |
| `docs/05-tasks/README.md` | executable actions | abstract goals |
| `docs/rules/` | project-wide engineering rules | product requirement details |

## Delivery Rules

- Do not start large implementation while blocking `[To confirm]` items remain unresolved.
- If there is a material ambiguity about requirements, architecture direction, stack choice, data model, or test coverage strategy, confirm it with the user before deciding.
- New features must update requirements, design, TDD, and tasks together.
- API, data shape, and config changes must update design docs and tests together.
- Bug fixes must include regression tests.
- New requirements, bug fixes, refactors, or workflow changes should also update `docs/changes/`.
- Releases or externally visible delivery changes should update `docs/releases/`.
- Unresolved issues, risks, and technical debt should update `docs/issues/`.
- Replaced or retired documents should be moved into `docs/archive/`.
- Any structure change must update the project tree in `README.md`.
- Record key technical trade-offs in `docs/adr/`.
- Every task should reference `FR-*`, `DES-*`, and `TEST-*` IDs.
- Mark uncertain information as `[To confirm]` instead of inventing it.
- Before implementation starts, ensure at least one complete chain exists: `FR -> DES -> TEST -> T`.
- When there is a more specific rule, follow the documents in `docs/rules/`.

